ssible way in which the owner of a building alleged to be unfit for human habitation can adequately protect his interest.", "S": "\"A predeprivation hearing after appropriate notice is the only possible way in which the owner of a building alleged to be unfit for human habitation can adequately protect his interest. Under the ordinance he may elect to contest the unfit characterization of the structure, or he can, by making repairs, save his property from destruction\", Ervin wrote."}, {"Q": "Can a postdeprivation hearing meet the owner's needs?", "A": "No", "S": "Hence, a postdeprivation hearing cannot possibly meet the owner's needs."}, {"Q": "What was the basis for limiting the time 35(b) petitions could be filed?", "A": "A finding that the Parole Commission was generally better suited to considering when prisoners could be released, which was found rational.", "S": "Becker further distinguished Logan from the instant case by noting that while the extinction of Logan's claim by the lapsed deadline in his FEPC claim frustrated the state's interest in fighting employment discrimination, Diggs could still address the issues that might make early release better for him and the government through parole applications."}, {"Q": "What was the result of the Supreme Court of Illinois's decision in the case?", "A": "The court unanimously held in favor of Zimmerman.", "S": "It conceded that the use of \"shall\" in the statute did not, by itself, make its provisions mandatory. \"One must consider the word within the context of the entire statute, looking particularly to the purpose of the legislation\", wrote Justice Daniel P. Ward for his colleagues."}, {"Q": "Can a postdeprivation hearing meet the needs of an individual who has been deprived of their rights?", "A": "No", "S": "\"Hence, a postdeprivation hearing cannot possibly meet the owner's needs.\""}, {"Q": "What was the main difference between the case in Parratt and the case in Logan?", "A": "New Jersey, unlike Nebraska, barred prisoners from suing under state tort law for the duration of their sentence.", "S": "New Jersey, unlike Nebraska, barred prisoners from suing under state tort law for the duration of their sentence."}, {"Q": "What was the court's decision regarding the deadline for filing a suit in Joshua v. City of Gainesville?", "A": "The court held that the rigid application of the deadline denied the petitioner's due process.", "S": "\"A claimant should not be penalized for attempting to allow a government agency to do its job.\""}, {"Q": "Why did the district court reject Shepherd of the Valley Lutheran Retirement Services' argument?", "A": "The court held that Shepherd had been aware of the deadline since Cesta's death and had waited too long to seek the appointment of a special administrator.", "S": "The trial court rejected that argument, holding that Shepherd had been aware of the deadline since Cesta's death, of which it was also aware, and had waited too long to seek the appointment of a special administrator."}, {"Q": "What was the deadline for complaints to be brought against an employer to the FEPC?", "A": "180 days from the alleged offense.", "S": "The FEPA set a deadline of 180 days from the alleged offense for complaints to be brought, and required only that FEPC notify any employer of a complaint against them within 120 days."}, {"Q": "What was the main reason for the trial court's decision to grant summary judgement to Cesta?", "A": "The trial court held that Shepherd had been aware of the deadline since Cesta's death and had waited too long to seek the appointment of a special administrator.", "S": "The trial court rejected that argument, holding that Shepherd had been aware of the deadline since Cesta's death, of which it was also aware, and had waited too long to seek the appointment of a special administrator."}, {"Q": "What did Justice John Paul Stevens see as a real equal protection concern?", "A": "A 120-day deadline that might allow claimants with spurious claims to get the full process while those with legitimate ones would not.", "S": "Justice John Paul Stevens saw \"a real equal protection concern\" with the 120-day deadline, since it might both allow claimants with spurious claims to get the full process while those with legitimate ones would not."}, {"Q": "Why did the Court characterize Logan as \"reiterat[ing]\" its earlier holding in Vitek v. Jones?", "A": "Because while states are free to create, modify or even abolish whatever procedures they wish for the remedy of constitutional wrongs, those procedures still must comply with federal law.", "S": "Justice Byron White's majority opinion characterized Logan as \"reiterat[ing]\" its earlier holding in Vitek v. Jones, that while states are free to create, modify or even abolish whatever procedures they wish for the remedy of constitutional wrongs, those procedures still must comply with federal law."}, {"Q": "Why did Justice Blackmun find the State's rationale for terminating claims dubious?", "A": "Because the State converts similarly situated claims into dissimilarly situated ones, and then uses this distinction as the basis for its classification, which is the very essence of arbitrary state action.", "S": "So far as the State's purpose is concerned, every FEPA claimant's charge, when filed with the Commission, stands on the same footing. Yet certain randomly selected claims, because processed too slowly by the State, are irrevocably terminated without review."}, {"Q": "Why was Holman's complaint dismissed in Mercer County Court?", "A": "Because of a New Jersey law that forbade prisoners from suing the government or its employees until they had completed their sentences and been released.", "S": "The complaint was dismissed per New Jersey law which forbade prisoners from suing the government or its employees until they had completed their sentences and been released."}, {"Q": "What opinion did Justice Harry Blackmun write for the Court?", "A": "Justice Harry Blackmun wrote that Logan's due process rights had been violated.", "S": "Justice Harry Blackmun wrote for the Court that Logan's due process rights had been violated."}, {"Q": "What was Justice Blackmun's opinion on the State's rationale for terminating claims?", "A": "Justice Blackmun believed that the State's rationale was arbitrary and lacked an objective basis.", "S": "\"The State's rationale must be something more than the exercise of a strained imagination; while the connection between means and ends need not be precise, it, at the least, must have some objective basis\", he concluded. \"That is not so here.\""}, {"Q": "What was the key factor in the Logan case that distinguished it from other cases?", "A": "The state process itself had been abused.", "S": "\"As in Logan, Bretz is challenging the direct abuse of the state process itself. It is meaningless to speak of the state's ability to provide postdeprivation remedial process where the state process itself has been abused\", Goodwin wrote."}, {"Q": "What is the key difference between the cases in Logan and those in Parratt?", "A": "Logan involves the direct abuse of the state process itself, whereas Parratt involves a random act.", "S": "\"As in Logan, Bretz is challenging the direct abuse of the state process itself. It is meaningless to speak of the state's ability to provide postdeprivation remedial process where the state process itself has been abused\", Goodwin wrote."}, {"Q": "What did Justice Blackmun argue about the nature of the Constitution's due process guarantee?", "A": "He argued that constitutional due process guarantees are federal and not diminished by the fact that the State may have specified its own procedures.", "S": "Justice Byron White had held there that constitutional due process guarantees are federal and \"not diminished by the fact that the State may have specified its own procedures that it may deem adequate for determining the preconditions to adverse official action\"."}, {"Q": "What was Justice Blackmun's main concern about the State's action in terminating FEPA claims?", "A": "The State converts similarly situated claims into dissimilarly situated ones, and then uses this distinction as the basis for its classification, which is arbitrary state action.", "S": "So far as the State's purpose is concerned, every FEPA claimant's charge, when filed with the Commission, stands on the same footing. Yet certain randomly selected claims, because processed too slowly by the State, are irrevocably terminated without review."}]}
